WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.920
Despite having been a graphics programmer
since the early 2000’s, the first time I

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.480
stepped into Horizon Forbidden West, I was
taken aback by the sheer scale of the world.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:17.710
How do games like this, or Assassin’s Creed,
or Spiderman, which also boast enormous, detailed

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.269
worlds, HOW do they draw all of this without
compromising on performance?

00:00:22.269 --> 00:00:27.920
It’s really interesting, because the techniques
used have been built on and refined for decades

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.420
at this point.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.059
So the first method is relatively simple.

00:00:32.059 --> 00:00:35.320
Let’s start with a world and populate it
with some stuff.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.160
It doesn’t really matter what that stuff
is, but we’ll keep this relatively simple.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.560
Now, let’s take a look from above, so let’s
pretend that we’re taking an eagle’s eye

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.480
view of what the player is seeing.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.020
So that’s the player down there, and we
can visualize what they see with this green

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.020
box.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.480
This is called the view frustum, it’s what
they can see from their perspective.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.829
And a view frustum is basically like a box.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:02.610
It’s defined by 6 sides, you have the left
and right, which of course correspond to the

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:07.210
left and right parts of your screen, you have
the top and bottom, which correspond to the

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:12.509
top and bottom of your screen, and finally
you have the near and far, the near being

00:01:12.509 --> 00:01:16.900
how close you can be to things before they
cut off, and the far being how far you can

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:17.900
see.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.729
Those 6 planes define everything that’s
visible to them.

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:23.659
This is called the viewing frustum.

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:28.990
On top of that, each object in the scene can
be thought of as having a simple volume around

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:34.000
them, sphere’s are popular because doing
math with sphere’s is really simple, things

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.439
like distance calculations, those sorts of
things, those are a breeze.

00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:42.390
You can get a much tighter fit with a box
though, the downside of course being that

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:47.680
the math tends to be a tiny bit more complex,
but not overly so, making them a nice tradeoff

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.880
on efficiency.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.369
Now that we have this idea of a viewing volume,
and each object having a simple bounding volume,

00:01:54.369 --> 00:01:59.040
then you’re simply going to do an intersection
test, for every single object in the scene,

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.039
you test whether their bounding volume intersects
with the view frustum, and everything that’s

00:02:04.039 --> 00:02:07.390
completely outside, that’s discarded, because
that’s not visible

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:11.840
And everything that’s inside or bordering,
that’s drawn.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.380
Remember this test needs to be conservative,
meaning that if you draw a little too much,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.690
that’s totally ok, because that’s better
than drawing too little.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.280
If we do that, here’s our scene, everything
that’s being discarded is being coloured

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.989
red, while everything that’s being kept
is being coloured green.

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:33.380
And as an example, if our world happens to
contain hundreds of objects, well, with this

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:38.290
simple pass, we’ve whittled that number
down considerably with just this simple technique.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:44.330
But frustum culling, as awesome as it is,
isn’t usually enough, as the recently released

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.620
Cities: Skylines 2 found out.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:50.879
Ignoring all the other problems with the game,
this report from Tom’s Hardware shows that

00:02:50.879 --> 00:02:54.909
they relied solely on frustum culling, which
is a really big mistake.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:02:58.330
It’s the first step, not the only step.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.959
You need to go further, and this is where
occlusion culling comes in.

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:07.290
Let’s take this simple example of a camera
and some objects sitting in the world.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:12.580
Now we can easily discard what’s blatantly
visible or not with our view frustum check.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:17.069
So everything outside of the viewing frustum,
we’ve already discarded those, being left

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:20.040
with just the things inside the view frustum.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.790
Now imagine a big object here, near the camera,
let’s say that it’s a wall, or a building,

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.050
or anything big and solid.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:32.170
You can’t see past this thing, so we can
draw lines from the camera to the edges of

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:38.930
this object, and extend them beyond, forming
a volume, an area where realistically, nothing

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:41.110
inside of it is visible to you.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:45.370
So that’s occlusion, but how do we calculate
occlusion?

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:49.209
And most importantly, how do games manage
to do it so fast?

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:52.019
Because this needs to be done near instantly.

00:03:52.019 --> 00:03:56.829
Let’s start with a really simple idea, that
occlusion culling isn’t all that different

00:03:56.829 --> 00:04:00.730
from frustum culling, at least in this simple
case here.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:05.239
In frustum culling, we’re getting rid of
what’s OUTSIDE of this area, defined by

00:04:05.239 --> 00:04:08.439
our view or our camera.

00:04:08.439 --> 00:04:14.040
Taking a superrrrrr simple approach to occlusion
culling, it’s almost like a reverse of that,

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.359
instead of getting rid of everything that’s
outside this area, we want to get rid of everything

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:20.340
that’s inside of it

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.360
Inside outside, outside inside

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.420
That in itself, isn’t all that complex,
and perhaps surprisingly, some big games in

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:32.810
the past have been shipped with this extremely
simple but effective approach.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:38.350
Basically, all that it entails is that somebody
goes into Maya, or whatever the 3D editing

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:42.750
software is, and they manually author occlusion
volumes.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:46.830
So let’s say that you have your object in
the world, you’ve got a big fat tree or

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:50.500
rock or whatever, doesn’t really matter
that much.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:56.259
Then an artist would secretly place a box,
or several boxes, inside, and these boxes

00:04:56.259 --> 00:04:59.960
aren’t visible to players, they’re only
used by the rendering system.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:05.650
Ideally, you’d place a box that fits pretty
snug inside, without spilling out.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.880
And you’d do this for every object in the
world that fits some sort of criteria, like

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.591
it’s big enough to be important, that sort
of thing.

00:05:12.591 --> 00:05:13.591
Artists didn’t have time to go and make
an occlusion volume for every pebble and twig

00:05:13.591 --> 00:05:14.591
on the ground, and you as an engine programmer
don’t want to have to deal with that much

00:05:14.591 --> 00:05:15.591
data anyway.

00:05:15.591 --> 00:05:17.160
So how is it that you actually perform occlusion
culling with one of these boxes?

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.300
Buncha ways, it’s not particularly complex.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:26.690
Let’s draw a border, and this will be our
screen, and now let’s show the box on the

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:31.970
screen, so we basically need to project the
box to screen space, which might sound fancy

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:34.759
but it just means draw it on the screen.

00:05:34.759 --> 00:05:36.150
See this silhouette here?

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:41.540
That’s defined by at least 4 sides, and
up to 6 sides, which you can create planes

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:45.980
out of, forming a viewing frustum, same as
we just dealt with for the camera.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:47.639
Except in reverse.

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:52.030
So now that you’ve got your data, your set
of all the occluders in the scene, and the

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:56.060
first thing you’re probably going to do
is a basic frustum culling pass on them, to

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.590
make sure they’re even relevant.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:01.930
This is where things get a little custom for
each approach, but basically you’re going

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:07.270
to pick a bunch of them, how you pick them
is kinda up to you, optimization is a game

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:13.311
of tradeoffs, you need to decide how much
time to burn in order to save time yeah, so

00:06:13.311 --> 00:06:14.311
it’s just an instance of that.

00:06:14.311 --> 00:06:17.830
Pick too many, and you may just spend way
too much time going through them.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:20.319
Pick too few, and you may not cull enough.

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:26.580
In practice, you may just do something super
simple, like pick the N closest ones and hope

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:33.789
for the best, which according to their presentation
from siggraph, is what Just Cause 2 did.

00:06:33.789 --> 00:06:40.699
The developers at Avalanche studios outlined
a simple and easy approach, with a SIMD optimized

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:47.259
box culling system, which they called BFBC
or brute force box culling, and that was it,

00:06:47.259 --> 00:06:49.639
no fancy structures, nothing.

00:06:49.639 --> 00:06:54.789
The very first game I worked on, Prototype,
did something really similar, if I’m remembering

00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:56.810
right, it’s been so long since I looked
at that code.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:57.810
So while those tree structures can be awesome
and theoretically faster, a well optimized,

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:58.810
hand written, SIMD brute force version may
be more than enough.

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:02.840
If you catch yourself wondering, wouldn’t
a tree be faster?

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:07.580
Watch my video “Memory, Cache Locality,
and why Arrays are Fast”.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:14.069
Theoretically the tree should win, but a brute
force SIMD optimized one may absolutely destroy

00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:16.240
it on modern hardware.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.020
So once you’ve got something like that in
place, we can place a simple occluder in the

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:24.780
scene, and bam, suddenly a lot of objects
that are behind it aren’t getting coloured

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:29.150
green anymore, they’re yellow, which in
this case means they are being occluded.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:34.970
It’s not perfect, and some things can slip
through the cracks, we can trivially set up

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:38.669
a situation that doesn’t work the way we
might expect it to.

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:45.199
Like here we have 2 occluders, side by side,
and 1 object behind them, but in the middle.

00:07:45.199 --> 00:07:50.790
Since neither of these occluders fully occludes
the object behind them individually, and we

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:55.870
don’t create a sort of union of occluders,
because that’s complicated, that object

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:57.979
gets flagged as drawn.

00:07:57.979 --> 00:08:01.120
So that’s it, that’s how modern games
do occlusion culling?

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:06.740
Well, you can use this approach if you need
a decent, but not absolute state of the art

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:07.900
system.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:12.169
What it does do though, is it served as a
great jumping off point to understand some

00:08:12.169 --> 00:08:13.889
of the other techniques.

00:08:13.889 --> 00:08:18.280
When you draw a scene, you don’t only generate
the colour that’s on the screen, you also

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:23.310
generate what’s called a depth buffer, and
this is really useful for the GPU.

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:28.259
What this does is keeps track of the depth
of every pixel on the screen, and this allows

00:08:28.259 --> 00:08:31.910
us, and the GPU, to do a variety of things.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:36.760
For the gpu, it allows it to do things like
render things in the right order, but also

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.010
discard things that aren’t visible.

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:43.760
If we didn’t have a depth buffer, the scene
would be a mess, parts of objects would draw

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.410
through others, and then you’d have to resort
to sorting the individual triangles of the

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:53.519
entire scene in order to have any hope of
drawing things somewhat ok.

00:08:53.519 --> 00:08:57.899
With a depth buffer, now the GPU can simply
compare the depth of what you’re rendering,

00:08:57.899 --> 00:09:04.209
against what’s already there, and if it’s
closer, it gets drawn, and if not, it doesn’t.

00:09:04.209 --> 00:09:08.990
For us, it’s pretty interesting too, because
we have a texture that basically has the depth

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:13.610
of the scene available, we can create effects
like this fire that doesn’t clip into the

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:18.940
scene, or even cheat and make really cheap
reflections, like we’re seeing in this water.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:24.630
The interesting part, in the context of occlusion
cullling, is how it allows us to discard entire

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.630
objects that aren’t visible.

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:32.610
If we already had a depth buffer drawn, let’s
ignore the problem of HOW do we generate one,

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:37.630
and let’s say that I can somehow magic one
into existence at the beginning of the frame,

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.560
how useful would it be?

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.010
The answer is of course, SUPER USEFUL.

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:47.640
If we SOMEHOW had that, we’d be able to
read from it, and do comparisons, so if I

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:52.100
wanted to check if I can draw an object, we
could take it’s bounding volume, and we

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:57.870
could take the screen space bounds of that,
map those to our depth buffer, and do a comparison

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:01.340
of the object’s depth in the scene with
the values in the depth buffer.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:06.440
If it’s behind the values in the depth buffer,
poof, we eliminate the entire object without

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:09.480
the gpu ever having done any work at all.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:15.530
In fact, we could go a step further, say I
take that depth buffer, and progressively

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:20.870
down sample it to form a mip chain, or hierarchy
of these occlusion maps.

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:25.600
Well then, when we project the screen space
bounds of our object, we could simply choose

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:31.540
the appropriate mip level so that we only
have to read 4 texels, and bam, an even faster

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:33.730
and easier comparison.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:39.720
These are called hierarchical z buffers, or
they’re often referred to as HZB’s, which

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:41.830
we’ll be doing from this point forward.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:48.050
So remember, HZB refers to this hierarchy
of z buffers, this sort of chain of progressively

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:49.490
downsampled occlusion maps.

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.850
But we have a chicken and egg situation here.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:57.220
All this depends on HAVING a depth buffer,
and that’s created as part of rendering

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:58.220
the scene.

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:02.450
But we need this to decide WHAT to render,
to save us work.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:04.839
Which is a bit of a problem, isn’t it?

00:11:04.839 --> 00:11:09.580
Early GPU’s were pretty restricted in what
they allowed you to do and not do, and one

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:13.470
thing that wasn’t easy was to read the depth
buffer back so that we could play with the

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.110
data ourselves.

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:20.120
One way around that is to not bother with
the GPU at all, and implement a software rasterizer.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:26.900
I mean, Quake came out in 1996 and required
a Pentium 75, so there’s no reason that

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:32.700
with vastly more powerful hardware, you shouldn’t
be able to spit out a few polygons on screen

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:34.310
really really quickly.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:38.360
And you can do all sorts of optimizations
here, remember the occlusion culling doesn’t

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:42.459
have to be perfect, it has to be good enough
and really fast.

00:11:42.459 --> 00:11:46.910
So one thing that you can do is instead of
drawing a full resolution buffer, you could

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:52.050
render to half resolution, or you can go even
further and go quarter resolution, i mean,

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:53.660
whatever works right

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:58.600
And you can hand build your occluders still,
well I mean have artists make them, and your

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:03.660
software rasterizer is basically only responsible
for drawing out something that would look

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.500
low poly even in the ps1 era.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:07.030
Now of course, doing it this way isn’t perfect,
but it gives you a bunch of advantages over

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:08.030
the previous, box only method.

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:09.030
But if you remember the example of the object
sitting between 2 occluders.

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:10.030
Well, using this new approach of generating
your occlusion map cpu side, the union of

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:11.030
occluders is not only possible, but a natural
consequence of the approach, as you can see

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:12.030
here.

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:13.030
We’ve got 2 occluders, side by side, and
they’re occluding the object behind it.

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:14.030
You also have a lot of flexibility in your
occluder shapes now, so say, a wall with a

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:15.030
window was now possible.

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:16.030
There wasn’t any good way to do that before,
but now it’s pretty easy to handle that

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:17.030
case, so this is huge progress.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:18.030
As we pan out, we can see that the window
is being taken into account for the occlusion

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:19.030
culling, which is amazing.

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:20.030
The approach itself isn’t that complex.

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:21.030
The initial steps of this are really similar
to what we had before, we do basic frustum

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:22.030
culling and such on the scene, and we also
get the list of occluders.

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:23.330
The new part is here, we have our new HZB,
and that can be whatever resolution works

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:25.079
good enough and fast.

00:12:25.079 --> 00:12:29.240
Then you’d draw all of your occluders into
that, and once that’s done you’ll go ahead

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:31.570
and downsample that to form the hierarchy.

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:36.300
Then you use that filled in hierarchy to manually
test objects to see if they’re visible or

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.010
not, and that’s it.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.660
Conceptually, it’s not super complex.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:44.710
So here we can see the HZB in action with
rendered objects.

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:49.230
In the top right corner, we’ve got on display
the top level mipmap, basically the depth

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:50.740
that was being drawn out.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:56.440
We’ve also got a few more levels of the
HZB underneath, at various mip levels.

00:12:56.440 --> 00:13:01.029
One of the really interesting things to take
note of is that, look at how, as you go towards

00:13:01.029 --> 00:13:06.770
the lowest resolution, the bright or “far
away” pixels are kinda taking over everything.

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:11.889
That’s because we do this conservatively,
as we downsample, we always pick the furthest

00:13:11.889 --> 00:13:13.290
away value.

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:17.570
Now of course, doing it this way isn’t perfect,
but it gives you a bunch of advantages over

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:20.199
the previous, box only method.

00:13:20.199 --> 00:13:25.250
Recall the example we looked at earlier, of
having an object sitting behind 2 occluders,

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:28.170
which failed miserably because of the approach
being used.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:33.620
Well, using this new approach of generating
your occlusion map cpu side, the union of

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:39.040
occluders is not only possible, but a natural
consequence of the approach, as you can see

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:40.040
here.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:45.810
We’ve got 2 occluders, side by side, and
they’re occluding the object behind it.

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:50.640
In fact, we can change this up and now we
can have a whole bunch of thin objects, lined

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:56.290
up side by side, and the overall net effect
of them is the same as having 1 giant wall.

00:13:56.290 --> 00:14:02.240
Look at the object behind, it now stays yellow,
the system is now reporting it as occluded.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:07.720
Or even better yet, we can see that using
non box shapes, like here I’ve got a disc,

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:11.980
and the technique just doesn’t care at all,
the shape of your objects, your occluders,

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:13.590
doesn’t matter in the slightest.

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:17.990
Here, we’re using the infamous stanford
bunny, but as an occluder.

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:22.590
The shape of your occluders no longer matters,
which is amazing, because it suddenly grants

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:26.500
this huge amount of flexibility that was lacking
before.

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:30.029
An example of a game that launched with this
was Killzone 3.

00:14:30.029 --> 00:14:36.430
In their Siggraph 2011 talk, Guerrilla Games
talks about using the SPU’s on the PS3.

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:41.700
Now if you’ve never done any PS3 development,
the PS3 shipped with what was called the Cell

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:49.140
Broadband Engine, under the hood you had a
powerpc main cpu, an nvidia gpu, and developers

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:55.990
had access to, I believe, 6 of the SPU’s
or Synergistic Processing Units, which were

00:14:55.990 --> 00:15:00.880
these stupidly powerful but difficult to use
vector processors.

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:06.209
Anyway, programming for them was a pain, but
if you had the right workload and a lot of

00:15:06.209 --> 00:15:09.800
patience, once you got them working, boy could
they do a lot.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:14.800
So Killzone 3 generated their occlusion map
using a simplified version of the scene, a

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:20.710
lower resolution depth buffer, and some highly
optimized SPU side rendering.

00:15:20.710 --> 00:15:27.190
Battlefield 3 gave a really similar talk at
gdc 2011, entitled Culling the Battlefield,

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:31.880
where they went into detail on a really similar
approach for the famous Frostbite engine used

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:33.660
in so many EA games.

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:39.090
But let’s be real, GPU’s are just wayyy
better at drawing than the CPU is, because

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:43.399
the raw horsepower they have is absolutely
unmatched.

00:15:43.399 --> 00:15:47.310
And that gap is only growing every year, not
getting smaller.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:52.310
Ideally then, what you want is that you want
to get the GPU to simply tell us what we want

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:55.800
to know, which is exactly what started happening.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:16:00.350
We started getting support for things like
hardware occlusion queries, a mechanism for

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:04.509
just asking the GPU, hey how much did you
draw?

00:16:04.509 --> 00:16:09.560
The idea is pretty simple, you could draw
part of your scene, like maybe just the major

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:10.560
occluders.

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:14.589
Then once you’ve done that, you want to
figure out all the small things, whether they’re

00:16:14.589 --> 00:16:20.029
visible or not, so you take the bounding volumes
of those objects, and issue queries to the

00:16:20.029 --> 00:16:22.130
GPU asking if they’re visible or not.

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:26.870
If they’re not visible, you can ignore them,
if they are, you can choose to render them

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:28.459
for real later.

00:16:28.459 --> 00:16:33.079
Articles like “Hardware Occlusion Queries
Made Useful” appeared in places like the

00:16:33.079 --> 00:16:38.040
GPU Gems series, that went into detail on
how you could use these queries to build very

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:41.200
general purpose occlusion culling systems.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:46.060
The team on Splinter Cell Conviction went
this route at first, ditching their precomputed

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:51.649
visibility system in favour of a new one built
around this newly exposed functionality.

00:16:51.649 --> 00:16:56.649
The problem was that issuing a lot of these
queries incurred cpu overhead, and you don’t

00:16:56.649 --> 00:16:58.300
get the answer immediately.

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:01.470
Both of these pose significant problems.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:02.852
If you want to cut down on queries, your first
idea is some sort of hierarchy of bounding

00:17:02.852 --> 00:17:03.852
volumes.

00:17:03.852 --> 00:17:04.852
So given a group of objects, maybe query on
the whole group first, that should save on

00:17:04.852 --> 00:17:05.852
CPU overhead.

00:17:05.852 --> 00:17:06.852
But that of course, runs head first into the
other issue, which is you don’t get the

00:17:06.852 --> 00:17:07.852
answer immediately.

00:17:07.852 --> 00:17:08.852
So you can stall the GPU by just waiting for
the answer, which is super easy, and just

00:17:08.852 --> 00:17:09.852
an awful idea.

00:17:09.852 --> 00:17:10.852
So let’s not do that.

00:17:10.852 --> 00:17:11.852
If you want to cut down on queries, your first
idea is probably some sort of hierarchy of

00:17:11.852 --> 00:17:12.852
bounding volumes.

00:17:12.852 --> 00:17:13.852
So you’d have some sort of hierarchy of
bounding volumes, where each node represents

00:17:13.852 --> 00:17:15.850
the bounding volume of all the children.

00:17:15.850 --> 00:17:19.950
So this root node, for example, is the bounding
volume of everything.

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:24.120
So given a node in the tree, you’d do a
query using that node’s combined bounding

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:28.690
volume, saving you the cpu overhead of having
to run a query on each object.

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:34.450
If that query fails, the entire subtree isn’t
visible, and you can safely move on.

00:17:34.450 --> 00:17:38.520
But that of course, runs head first into the
other issue, which is you don’t get the

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:39.520
answer immediately.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:46.179
So you could just stall and wait for the answer,
which is super easy, and just an awful idea.

00:17:46.179 --> 00:17:50.050
You don’t want the GPU or CPU just sitting
there idle.

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:54.690
So then you come up with various attempts
to mitigate THAT problem, like Nvidia was

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:59.880
proposing here, but ultimately, it’s a whole
can of worms and difficult to get working

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:04.550
well, which is why the Splinter Cell team
just wholly abandoned all the work they did

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:06.370
and went in another direction.

00:18:06.370 --> 00:18:11.110
Late in the project, they pivoted away from
their query based system to something that’s

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:13.630
getting a lot closer to what’s in use today.

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:19.030
They take their set of occluders, and remember
these can be simplified versions of existing

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:22.140
geometry, and render them out to a depth buffer.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:26.330
Now they have a depth buffer, and they use
that to build a depth hierarchy, similar to

00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:28.120
what we showed before.

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:33.159
So each level is half the resolution of the
last, and you take the maximum depth of the

00:18:33.159 --> 00:18:37.440
4 texels from the higher resolution map that
turn into 1 in the lower resolution.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:43.250
Then, they take everything they want to test
and draw that as a set of points, so each

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:48.150
point knows the screen space box extents of
the object they want to test, and the depth

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:54.020
it’s testing, and it’s responsible for
outputting a pixel with visible or not visible.

00:18:54.020 --> 00:18:59.370
Then that final, single render target is read
back from the GPU to the CPU, and now they

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:01.940
can see the results of their queries.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:05.980
And the awesome thing about this approach
was that, most of it happened on the GPU,

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:09.340
there’s only this single stall at the end
reading back.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:14.840
But in reality, renderers have a crapload
of work to do on the CPU as well, so if you

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:19.430
can front-load this occlusion work early in
your frame, meaning you do it as early in

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:25.580
your frame update as you can, then shove whatever
other bookkeeping work you can find, let the

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:30.230
GPU do it’s thing, and then do your single
read back afterwards, then it’s not the

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:31.460
end of the world.

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:35.840
One set of problems that we’d like to get
away from though is this manual work step

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:40.500
of authoring occlusion geometry, and having
to select occluders to use.

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:46.760
That’s time being spent that could be spent
on making more content, polish, etc.

00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:51.760
One observation that you can make about any
given scene is that, from one frame to the

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.909
next, very little changes.

00:19:53.909 --> 00:19:59.960
In reality, to generate the current frame,
we mostly take the last frame and mutate it

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:01.799
a tiny bit.

00:20:01.799 --> 00:20:08.570
Objects might be moving around, but at 60fps,
for example, that translates into barely anything.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:12.090
Same with the camera, that will have moved
but only every so slightly.

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:15.100
What if we could exploit this somehow?

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:19.440
One attempt to exploit this temporal coherence
was described by the developers from Assassin’s

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:20.440
Creed.

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:21.440
Full disclosure, whether or not they came
up with it first, I have no idea, but it’s

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:22.440
where I saw it talked about.

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:25.360
Their approach seemed to be to take a bunch
of the nearby stuff and render it into a depth

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:26.360
buffer.

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:31.370
Then, they’d take last frame’s depth buffer,
reproject that to the current frame, and combine

00:20:31.370 --> 00:20:32.809
those 2 together.

00:20:32.809 --> 00:20:34.549
Wait, what’s reprojection?

00:20:34.549 --> 00:20:36.440
The idea isn’t overly complex.

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:41.760
If you know roughly where things were last
frame, where the camera was, and which direction

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:46.429
things were moving, then you can re-project,
or in other words, guess where they should

00:20:46.429 --> 00:20:47.429
be this frame.

00:20:47.429 --> 00:20:48.429
That’s all.

00:20:48.429 --> 00:20:53.350
Anyway, so they describe using this approach
of combining the old and new, creating the

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:57.540
HZB from that, and then doing their occlusion
queries.

00:20:57.540 --> 00:20:59.440
So that’s really a neat approach.

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:09.600
But unfortunately, quite difficult to manage
properly, with a lot of special casing in

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:14.299
the engine and implementation in order to
accommodate this approach.

00:21:14.299 --> 00:21:19.230
The basic assumption, that stuff doesn’t
change too much from frame to frame, that’s

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:22.620
still good, but the technique wasn’t quite
right.

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:27.960
So that leads us to more or less what’s
being used today in engines like Unreal.

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:33.679
The basic idea is that all the stuff that
passed the visibility test last frame, they’re

00:21:33.679 --> 00:21:40.530
very likely visible this frame, so take those
probably visible objects, and use them as

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:41.530
occluders.

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:46.690
So we draw all of those, and then we take
the depth or z buffer and we build the HZB.

00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:51.380
So, if you’re wondering, this doesn’t
seem totally right, because let’s say that

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:55.590
an object was off screen before, and now is
suddenly on-screen.

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:58.820
They should be occluding things, but that’s
not happening right?

00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:03.810
That’s ok, this approach is conservative,
you might overdraw a bit.

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:07.289
Like I said before, that’s better than missing
things.

00:22:07.289 --> 00:22:12.150
Secondly, because it was off-screen, and we
only drew things that were on-screen, we didn’t

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:13.440
even draw this new object.

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:18.990
Well, that’s a problem, so we need to move
onto the 2nd phase of this algorithm.

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:23.929
This approach is known as 2 pass occlusion
culling because, well, it has 2 passes.

00:22:23.929 --> 00:22:25.600
It’s right there in the name.

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:27.470
It’s not a mystery.

00:22:27.470 --> 00:22:31.600
In this 2nd pass, we’re going to go over
everything that wasn’t drawn the first time,

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:36.510
and we’re going to re-test it against the
HZB that we built in step 1.

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:40.240
So that big shiny new object that moved into
the middle of the screen?

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:43.600
That will obviously pass the test, and get
drawn.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:48.409
And because nowadays we have compute shaders,
which have the ability to generate the arguments

00:22:48.409 --> 00:22:54.150
for draw calls themselves, this entire pipeline,
all of these steps, takes place on the GPU,

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:55.150
which is awesome.

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:56.880
What does the future bring?

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:01.700
I’m not sure, there’s really exciting
work being presented where individual objects

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:07.520
are now being broken apart and occlusion culled,
which is a logical next step, and then you

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:13.809
have technology like Nanite from Unreal engine,
which is something of an automatic level of

00:23:13.809 --> 00:23:14.809
detail system.

00:23:14.809 --> 00:23:16.039
Very cool stuff.

00:23:16.039 --> 00:23:21.290
Be under no illusions that this is a complete
picture of how visibility is done, but it

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:26.179
should give you a pretty decent overview of
what’s used today, and how we got there.

00:23:26.179 --> 00:23:31.020
We haven’t touched on things like precomputed
visibility, or other aspects of just making

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.470
the scene cheaper in general to render.

00:23:32.470 --> 00:23:33.470
I can take this scene, and with very little
in the way of visible differences, the scene

00:23:33.470 --> 00:23:34.470
can suddenly render significantly faster.

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:37.860
There’s so much work that goes into modern
rendering, and we’ve just started scratching

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:39.179
the surface.

00:23:39.179 --> 00:23:39.450
Cheers

